{
  "_comment": "Guard states that aibuild-lab/workshop-installer installed directly. Its SETUP-PROMPT step 5.3.5 (and, from 09-04-2026, its install.mjs) put hooks/secrets-guard.js, hooks/secrets-tripwire.js and hooks/install.mjs from that repository's main branch into ~/.claude/hooks (Claude only, no Codex files, no adapters), so a student holds whichever bytes were on main the day they set up. Each record is one state of those three files on workshop-installer main (first-parent history), with the commit that put it there. States that agent-native-os also pinned are already in secrets-guard.course-deployed.identities.json or secrets-guard.legacy.identities.json and are not repeated; this file holds only the workshop states no other record names. Every workshop main state (the six here and the seven named elsewhere) also has a CRLF record (lineEndings \"crlf\", ref ending -crlf). workshop-installer has no .gitattributes, so Git for Windows with its default core.autocrlf=true checks those files out with CRLF line endings, and the workshop install.mjs copies them unchanged. Each CRLF record holds the exact bytes of a core.autocrlf=true checkout at that commit (every LF preceded by CR, nothing else different), and sameContentAs names the reviewed LF record with the same content. They match by exact hash like every other record: no line-ending normalization, so an edited copy, mixed line endings or a CRLF file beside an LF file still holds. Reviewed bytes establish identity only: a receipt-free installation is attributed only when all three files belong to the same record, and it still needs a separate human approval. refresh-guard.mjs pins this file's LF-normalized sha256 (WORKSHOP_INSTALLER_IDENTITIES_SHA256); change both in one reviewed pull request, and add a record only with its source commit proven.",
  "sourceRepository": "aibuild-lab/workshop-installer",
  "sourcePaths": [
    "hooks/secrets-guard.js",
    "hooks/secrets-tripwire.js",
